The `CaseConverter.execute` method calls `.upper()`, `.lower()`, `.capitalize()`, `.title()` without `None` validation.

```python
def execute(self, string, mode, **kwargs):
    if mode == "UPPERCASE":
        result = string.upper()
    elif mode == "lowercase":
        result = string.lower()
    elif mode == "Capitalize":
        result = string.capitalize()
    elif mode == "Title Case":
        result = string.title()
```

If `string` is `None`, these method calls will raise `AttributeError`.

Post-context evidence: Lines 64-76 (CaseConverter.execute) call .upper(), .lower(), etc. on string without null checks. The else clause at line 75 returns string as-is, but if string is None, this also fails. The digest confirms STRING inputs can accept None if not connected or explicitly set. This is a concrete defect - if any upstream node passes None, all CaseConverter operations crash. Recommendation to add null check is valid.
